Cross-compiling to another OPSYS: allow USE_TOOLS+=...:run when the
native and target layouts agree.

A tool used at run time becomes a DEPENDS of the package, and its path
is substituted into the package (interpreter lines, REPLACE_*, scripts)
from the native build. The check refuses that whenever the target OPSYS
differs from the native one, because then the native path need not
exist on the target.

What actually matters is that the paths agree, not that the OS names
do. A cross build whose native pkgsrc lives at the target's LOCALBASE
(/usr/pkg on both sides) is in the same position as a NetBSD -> NetBSD
build, which the check already allows. TOOLS_CROSS_RUN_SAME_PATHS=yes
in mk.conf says so; without it nothing changes.

--- mk/tools/replace.mk
+++ mk/tools/replace.mk
@@ -141,6 +141,7 @@
 .endfor
 .for _t_ in ${USE_TOOLS:M*\:run}
-.  if ${USE_CROSS_COMPILE:tl} == "yes" && ${OPSYS} != ${NATIVE_OPSYS}
+.  if ${USE_CROSS_COMPILE:tl} == "yes" && ${OPSYS} != ${NATIVE_OPSYS} && \
+      ${TOOLS_CROSS_RUN_SAME_PATHS:Uno:tl} != "yes"
 PKG_FAIL_REASON+=	"USE_TOOLS+=${_t_} not supported in cross-compilation"
 .  endif
 _TOOLS_DEPMETHOD.${_t_:C/:.*//}=	DEPENDS
